---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import BookingLink from "../../components/BookingLink.astro";
const title = "PHP2Shell: What Happened and How to Fix It | Truva Solutions";
const description =
  "PHP2Shell explained simply: who is affected, the four fixed PHP versions, what a firewall can and cannot do, and a practical response checklist.";
const contents = [
  ["simple-explanation", "The problem, simply"],
  ["who-is-affected", "Who is affected?"],
  ["fixed-versions", "Which update do you need?"],
  ["response-plan", "What to do now"],
  ["temporary-protection", "If you cannot patch yet"],
  ["investigation", "Check for a break-in"],
  ["references", "References"],
];
const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    headline: "PHP2Shell: the problem and the fix",
    description,
    datePublished: "2026-10-21",
    dateModified: "2026-10-21",
    author: {
      "@type": "Organization",
      name: "Truva Solutions",
      url: "https://truvasolutions.com",
    },
    publisher: { "@id": "https://truvasolutions.com/#organization" },
    image: "https://truvasolutions.com/images/php2shell-explained.png",
    mainEntityOfPage: "https://truvasolutions.com/blog/php2shell-explained",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  extraSchema={schema}
  ogImage="/images/php2shell-explained.png"
  ogImageAlt="PHP2Shell explained: a web request, vulnerable PHP worker, and the update that fixes it"
  ogType="article"
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main" class="php-article">
    <header class="article-hero shell">
      <a class="article-back" href="/resources">
        Back to Resources
      </a>
      <p class="eyebrow">APPLICATION SECURITY · EXPLAINED SIMPLY</p>
      <h1>
        PHP2Shell: <span>the problem and the fix.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="article-deck">
        A visitor should be able to ask your website a question. They should
        never be able to take over the computer behind it. Two newly disclosed
        PHP flaws can cross that boundary.
      </p>
      <div class="article-meta">
        <span>Truva Solutions</span>
        <span>6 min read</span>
        <time datetime="2026-10-21">21 October 2026</time>
      </div>
      <p class="update-note">
        Checked against primary advisories on 21 October 2026. This is a
        developing incident; check your software vendor for newer updates.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="article-layout shell">
      <article class="article-body" aria-label="PHP2Shell explained">
        <figure class="lead-visual">
          <img
            src="/images/php2shell-explained.svg"
            width="1200"
            height="675"
            alt="A web visitor sends a request to a vulnerable PHP worker. Updating PHP repairs the worker before requests are processed."
            fetchpriority="high"
          />
          <figcaption>
            The flaw is inside PHP, the software doing the work behind a
            website.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <details class="mobile-contents">
          <summary>Table of contents</summary>
          <nav aria-label="Mobile table of contents">
            {contents.map(([id, label], i) => (
              <a href={`#${id}`}>
                {i + 1}. {label}
              </a>
            ))}
          </nav>
        </details>
        <div class="takeaway">
          <strong>The short answer</strong>
          <p>
            Find every PHP web server, install the security update, and restart
            the workers that handle web requests. A website firewall can help
            while you patch. It does not repair PHP.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="fact-strip" aria-label="Verified facts at a glance">
          <div>
            <strong>2</strong>
            <span>critical vulnerabilities</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>9.2 / 10</strong>
            <span>CVSS v4.0 score for each</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>4</strong>
            <span>supported PHP branches patched</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="fact-source">
          CVSS describes severity, not the chance of an attack. Release and
          severity sources: PHP maintainers and NHS England.{" "}
          <a href="#references" aria-label="See primary references">
            References
          </a>
        </p>
        <section id="simple-explanation">
          <h2>1. The problem, simply</h2>
          <p>
            Imagine a little shop. A customer asks for a sandwich. A helper
            reads the order, makes the sandwich, and clears the counter for the
            next customer.
          </p>
          <p>
            For many websites, PHP is that helper. It reads requests, runs the
            website’s code, and helps return a page.
          </p>
          <p>
            These two bugs affect how the helper manages its work and clears up
            after something goes wrong. A carefully made request can leave PHP
            using unfinished information or information it should have thrown
            away.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The dangerous result:</strong> someone outside the website
            may be able to make the server run their code. Security teams call
            this <strong>remote code execution</strong>, or RCE. It can put the
            data and permissions available to that PHP process at risk.
          </p>
          <div class="plain-note">
            <strong>The fix in the shop example</strong>
            <p>
              Give the helper a corrected set of working instructions. In a real
              system, that means updating PHP itself. Changing the shop’s sign,
              website theme, or login password does not fix this flaw.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section id="who-is-affected">
          <h2>2. Who is affected?</h2>
          <p>
            PHP’s maintainers warn about installations serving web requests,
            including PHP-FPM, Apache with mod_php, php-cgi, FrankenPHP, and
            LiteSpeed. They identify{" "}
            <strong>mod_php as the highest-risk setup</strong>, with a
            demonstrated attack that can trigger before the website’s own code
            runs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check websites built with WordPress, Drupal, Joomla, Laravel, and
            other PHP software. Their presence is a reason to check the
            underlying PHP runtime, not proof that a particular website has been
            hacked.
          </p>
          <div
            class="table-scroll"
            role="region"
            tabindex="0"
            aria-label="The two PHP2Shell vulnerabilities"
          >
            <table>
              <caption>
                Two different bugs, with the same urgent update action
              </caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Vulnerability</th>
                  <th>What goes wrong</th>
                  <th>Plain meaning</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <strong>CVE-2026-109618</strong>
                    <br />
                    Out-of-memory corruption
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    PHP may stop before request data is fully prepared, then use
                    that unfinished data during cleanup.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The helper clears a half-finished order as though it were
                    ready.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <strong>CVE-2026-109619</strong>
                    <br />
                    Stale global state
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    PHP may keep a reference to request memory after that memory
                    has been released.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The helper follows an old note that should have been
                    discarded.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Attack activity is already being reported. Cloudflare says it
            observed traffic targeting its customers. AWS reports an attempt
            against its MadPot sensors on <strong>17 October</strong>, before
            public disclosure. That observation does not establish that the
            attempt succeeded against a real customer system.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="fixed-versions">
          <h2>3. Which update do you need?</h2>
          <p>
            Use the fixed release for your supported PHP branch, or a newer
            supported security release. If your Linux or hosting vendor
            backports fixes, check its advisory and patched package build
            instead of judging safety from the upstream version number alone.
          </p>
          <div
            class="table-scroll"
            role="region"
            tabindex="0"
            aria-label="PHP2Shell fixed versions"
          >
            <table>
              <caption>Upstream releases that fix both vulnerabilities</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Your PHP branch</th>
                  <th>First fixed release</th>
                  <th>What to do</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>PHP 8.5</td>
                  <td>
                    <strong>8.5.12</strong>
                  </td>
                  <td>Update to this release or newer.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>PHP 8.4</td>
                  <td>
                    <strong>8.4.27</strong>
                  </td>
                  <td>Update to this release or newer.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>PHP 8.3</td>
                  <td>
                    <strong>8.3.36</strong>
                  </td>
                  <td>Update to this release or newer.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>PHP 8.2</td>
                  <td>
                    <strong>8.2.35</strong>
                  </td>
                  <td>Update now and plan the next supported branch.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>PHP 8.1 and older</td>
                  <td>No routine upstream support</td>
                  <td>
                    Move to a supported branch, or obtain a confirmed fix from
                    your support vendor.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not stop at “WordPress is up to date.”</strong> Your CMS
            and PHP have separate updates. Ask your host which PHP build is
            actually running for the website.
          </p>
        </section>
        <figure class="response-visual">
          <img
            src="/images/php2shell-response.svg"
            width="1200"
            height="675"
            loading="lazy"
            alt="Four steps: find the PHP runtime, update it, restart all workers, and verify both service health and security evidence."
          />
          <figcaption>
            Close the task with evidence from the running service, not only an
            update command.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <section id="response-plan">
          <h2>4. What to do now</h2>
          <p>
            Give one person responsibility for the response. Include production
            websites, admin panels, old subdomains, containers, and spare
            servers that still receive traffic.
          </p>
          <div
            class="table-scroll"
            role="region"
            tabindex="0"
            aria-label="PHP2Shell response ownership and evidence"
          >
            <table>
              <caption>A practical response checklist</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Step</th>
                  <th>Action</th>
                  <th>Evidence to keep</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <strong>1. Find</strong>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The application owner and host identify the PHP runtime,
                    package source, and every exposed instance.
                  </td>
                  <td>A list of affected services with a named owner.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <strong>2. Update</strong>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The platform team installs a verified patched package or
                    rebuilds the container image.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The vendor advisory and installed package or image
                    identifier.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <strong>3. Restart</strong>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    Replace or restart every affected PHP worker. Use a
                    controlled rollout for busy services.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    Confirmation that old workers and old containers are gone.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <strong>4. Verify</strong>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    Check the runtime serving the website, test key user
                    journeys, and review security signals.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    Runtime build, test results, and the reviewed log window.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <code>php -v</code> can help identify the command-line version.{" "}
            <strong>
              It does not prove the website uses that same PHP build.
            </strong>{" "}
            Confirm the web worker or container too. Do not leave a public{" "}
            <code>phpinfo()</code> page behind as evidence.
          </p>
          <p>
            If a hosting company manages PHP, ask: “Have you patched both CVEs,
            restarted the affected workers, and checked every PHP site on this
            account?” Request the package build and completion time.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="temporary-protection">
          <h2>5. If you cannot patch yet</h2>
          <p>
            Reduce exposure while arranging the update. Restrict access to the
            affected service, and consider taking it offline if you cannot make
            the risk acceptable. Test restrictions against the real application
            so a rushed change does not break essential functions.
          </p>
          <p>
            A web application firewall, or WAF, is a filter in front of the
            website. Cloudflare reports default protections for customers using
            its WAF, including its Free plan.{" "}
            <strong>
              Check that traffic actually passes through the protection and
              cannot reach the origin directly.
            </strong>{" "}
            Another provider may have different coverage.
          </p>
          <p>
            PHP’s advisory also lists temporary configuration changes for
            particular attack paths. These include upload-related settings and
            worker restarts.{" "}
            <strong>There is no complete configuration-only fix.</strong> Have
            the server owner follow the current maintainer advisory for the
            exact runtime. Avoid copying isolated settings from social media.
          </p>
          <div class="plain-note">
            <strong>A filter buys time. An update repairs the software.</strong>
            <p>
              Do not mark the incident resolved just because the WAF blocked
              some requests.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section id="investigation">
          <h2>6. Check for a break-in</h2>
          <p>
            Patching closes a weakness. It does not remove an attacker who may
            already be inside.
          </p>
          <p>
            AWS recommends reviewing web logs, unexpected process execution, and
            file changes. Its earliest reported sensor activity was on 17
            October, so include that date in your review when retained logs
            allow. Preserve relevant logs and evidence before rebuilding a
            suspicious system.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Look for unexpected files, changed application code, and unusual
              scheduled jobs.
            </li>
            <li>
              Review processes started by the web worker and unexplained
              outbound connections.
            </li>
            <li>
              If compromise is suspected, isolate the system and involve
              incident responders. Identify and rotate exposed secrets from a
              clean environment.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A quiet dashboard is not proof of safety. Record what you checked,
            what logs were available, and what remains unknown.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="references" class="references">
          <h2>7. References</h2>
          <p>
            Primary sources used for this article. Release and protection
            details can change as the response develops.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <a href="https://www.php.net/archive/2026.php#2026-10-21-5">
                PHP: critical security advisory and fixed releases, 21 October
                2026
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w">
                PHP maintainer advisory: CVE-2026-109618
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp">
                PHP maintainer advisory: CVE-2026-109619
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/php-vulnerabilities-waf-protection/">
                Cloudflare: observed attack traffic and PHP2Shell WAF protection
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/amazon-threat-intelligence-identifies-php2shell-exploitation-prior-to-public-disclosure-in-madpot/">
                AWS: PHP2Shell activity observed in MadPot telemetry
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://digital.nhs.uk/cyber-alerts/2026/cc-4875">
                NHS England: affected versions, severity scores, and remediation
              </a>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>
        <section class="article-next">
          <h2>Know what is exposed. Prove what is fixed.</h2>
          <p>
            Truva helps teams review application exposure and turn security
            findings into a clear remediation plan.
          </p>
          <BookingLink label="Discuss your application security" />
        </section>
      </article>
      <aside class="article-sidebar">
        <nav aria-label="Table of contents">
          <p class="eyebrow">TABLE OF CONTENTS</p>
          {contents.map(([id, label], i) => (
            <a href={`#${id}`}>
              <span>{i + 1}</span>
              {label}
            </a>
          ))}
        </nav>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Base>
